    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-3.3.06-04",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "Water pollution can arise where sewage, industrial wastes, fuel or runoff enter sources without suitable control. Hard surfaces can route water rapidly, carrying pollutants towards drains or channels. Inadequate sanitation can create exposure pathways, while treatment and source protection can reduce them. A drain's presence does not establish effective treatment; it may only move water elsewhere. Quantity and quality require separate observations.",
      "claim_ids": [
