    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-3.3.06-10",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "Out-of-town shopping areas can use larger sites and parking, while affecting central trade or increasing car dependence if alternatives are weak. Sports facilities may need open land and transport access, while industrial estates need freight and service infrastructure. These uses can cluster at the fringe for different reasons. Their location should be explained through land, access and function rather than just called sprawl without a mechanism.",
