    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-3.3.06-15",
      "block_type": "worked_application",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "Urbanisation and sprawl can move differently. Suppose the urban population share rises while new residents occupy existing buildings or appropriate infill sites. The urban share can increase without a comparable extension of the footprint. Conversely, a low-density edge estate or new industrial park can expand built area even where the urban share changes little. Distinguishing the measures prevents a spatial map from being used as proof of a demographic rate.",
      "claim_ids": [
        "CL-6137-3.3.06-1",
        "CL-6137-3.3.06-2",
        "CL-6137-3.3.06-01-1",
