    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-3.3.06-18",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "Air-quality comparisons should use suitable observations and conditions. A lower pollutant reading on a windy day can partly reflect dispersion rather than reduced emissions. A higher roadside reading may reflect traffic, weather or other nearby sources. A count, a concentration and a health outcome are different measures. A supported conclusion should not replace them with one another or assign a clinical cause from a simple field observation.",
      "claim_ids": [
