    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-3.3.06-21",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "Outward development can also raise infrastructure lengths and service costs. Longer pipes, roads or collection routes may be needed for scattered edge uses, depending on layout and existing capacity. A new centre may create local access while drawing journeys from a wider area. Compare the relevant network and users before assuming all edge development is inefficient. Some designs may connect effectively to existing routes and mixed uses.",
